<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>The ASPCA lists poinsettias as toxic to cats, dogs and horses, but calls them “generally over-rated in toxicity”.</li>
<li>Typical signs are drooling and vomiting, which usually settle without treatment.</li>
<li>The sap can also irritate skin and, rarely, eyes.</li>
<li>The “deadly poinsettia” idea traces to an unproven 1919 story.</li>
<li>True lilies are a far bigger holiday risk for cats.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Are Poinsettias Toxic to Cats? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>Poinsettias are mildly toxic to cats. Their sap irritates the mouth and stomach, so a cat that bites a leaf may drool or vomit, but the signs are usually short-lived and rarely need treatment. A poinsettia is not a reason to panic, though it is still sensible to keep it out of reach.</p>
<p>Pet Poison Helpline gives the poinsettia a mild rating for both cats and dogs.</p>

<h3>What is a poinsettia, and why is its sap irritating?</h3>
<p><em>Euphorbia pulcherrima</em> is a shrub in the spurge family (Euphorbiaceae), native to southern Mexico and Central America according to NC State Extension. Its coloured “petals” are bracts, or modified leaves. Broken stems release a milky latex that, per Pet Poison Helpline, contains diterpenoid euphorbol esters and saponin-like detergents, which irritate the mouth, stomach and skin.</p>

<h2>Is a Poinsettia Toxic to Dogs?</h2>
<p>A poinsettia is mildly toxic to dogs, just as it is to cats. A dog that chews the plant may drool, vomit or have a short bout of stomach upset. Because dogs can swallow more plant material at once, they may feel worse than a cat that took a single bite, but serious problems are still uncommon.</p>
<p>If your dog is a determined chewer, our <a href="/tools/pet-proof-home-guide/">room-by-room pet-proofing guide</a> has ideas for keeping plants and decorations out of reach.</p>

<h2>Are Poinsettias Poisonous to Cats and Dogs? Signs to Watch For</h2>
<p>Poinsettias are mildly poisonous to cats and dogs. Signs usually start soon after chewing: drooling, lip-licking, pawing at the mouth and vomiting, with diarrhoea less common. Pet Poison Helpline also lists skin and eye irritation from contact with the sap.</p>

<table class="pz-compare-table">
<thead>
<tr><th>How your pet was exposed</th><th>What you might see</th><th>How serious</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Chewed or swallowed leaves</td><td>Drooling, lip-licking, pawing at the mouth, vomiting</td><td>Usually mild</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swallowed a larger amount</td><td>Repeated vomiting, sometimes diarrhoea, not wanting to eat</td><td>Mild to moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sap on the skin</td><td>Redness, swelling or itching where the sap touched</td><td>Usually mild</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sap in the eye</td><td>Mild eye irritation (rare)</td><td>Usually mild</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Pet Poison Helpline notes there is no antidote, but signs usually resolve on their own and rarely need treatment unless severe or persistent. If your cat keeps being sick, our <a href="/tools/cat-vomiting-guide/">cat vomiting guide</a> explains when vomiting becomes a reason to see a vet.</p>

<h3>Poinsettia sap: skin and eye irritation</h3>
<p>Poinsettia sap can make skin red, puffy or itchy, and occasionally irritates the eyes. It only escapes when stems or leaves break, so brushing past an intact plant is unlikely to cause problems. Wash skin with mild soap and water. For eyes, rinse gently with clean water and call your vet if your pet keeps squinting or pawing at the eye.</p>

<h2>What to Do If a Cat Eats a Poinsettia</h2>
<p>If a cat eats a poinsettia, take the plant away, wipe any leaf pieces from its mouth, offer fresh water and watch for a few hours. Mild drooling or one episode of vomiting is common and usually passes. Call your vet or a poison line if signs are severe, keep going, or you are unsure which plant it was.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Remove the plant</strong> and fallen leaves.</li>
<li><strong>Wipe plant pieces from the mouth</strong> and offer fresh water.</li>
<li><strong>Wash sap off fur or skin</strong> with mild soap and water.</li>
<li><strong>Do not try to make your pet vomit</strong> or give home remedies or medicines. Leave treatment decisions to a vet.</li>
<li><strong>Watch your pet</strong> for the next few hours.</li>
<li><strong>Call your vet</strong> if vomiting or diarrhoea continues, your pet will not eat or drink, seems very tired, or you are not sure which plant it was.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you cannot reach your vet, an animal poison helpline can advise you. In the United States, the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center is (888) 426-4435 and Pet Poison Helpline is (855) 764-7661. Both are open 24 hours a day, and a fee may apply. Our <a href="/tools/pet-poison-emergency-guide/">pet poison emergency guide</a> lists what to have ready when you call.</p>

<h3>What to do if a dog eats a poinsettia</h3>
<p>If a dog eats a poinsettia, follow the same steps as for a cat: remove the plant, clear the mouth, offer water and watch for drooling or vomiting. Also check whether it swallowed the pot, foil or soil.</p>
<p>Call your vet if your dog ate a large part of the plant or its packaging, vomits repeatedly or stops eating. Our list of <a href="/blog/signs-your-pet-is-sick/">warning signs that your pet is sick</a> can help you decide whether a symptom needs attention.</p>

<h2>Is a Poinsettia Toxic to Birds and Other Animals?</h2>
<p>Poinsettia is toxic to pets beyond cats and dogs: it appears on veterinary lists of plants that can harm birds, and the ASPCA lists it as toxic to horses. Keep poinsettias out of rooms where a bird flies free, and call a vet if a bird eats one.</p>
<p>The Merck Veterinary Manual’s guide for bird owners names poinsettia, mistletoe and calla lilies among plants that may poison birds. VCA Animal Hospitals also lists poinsettia, while noting that research on plant safety in birds is limited. For other household dangers to parrots and budgies, see our <a href="/tools/bird-toxic-foods-guide/">foods toxic to birds list</a>.</p>

<h2>Are Poinsettias Poisonous to Humans, Kids or Babies?</h2>
<p>Poinsettias are not considered poisonous to humans, including children and babies. Eating the leaves may cause a stomach ache or vomiting, and the sap can irritate skin in people with a latex allergy. Even so, University of Wisconsin–Madison Extension advises that children and pets should not be allowed to eat any indoor plants.</p>
<p>University of Illinois Extension cites an Ohio State University study estimating that a 50-pound child would need to eat around 500 to 600 leaves, or more than 1¼ pounds of plant, before any harmful effects. If a child eats part of a plant, US families can call Poison Help on 1-800-222-1222.</p>

<h3>Where did the poinsettia myth come from?</h3>
<p>The poinsettia myth comes from a 1919 story about a two-year-old child who reportedly died after eating a poinsettia leaf. According to University of Illinois Extension, the claim was never proven and was later judged to be hearsay.</p>

<h2>What Holiday Plants Are Poisonous to Cats and Dogs?</h2>
<p>The holiday plants most poisonous to cats are true lilies and daylilies, which can cause kidney failure. The holiday plants poisonous to dogs and cats alike include mistletoe and amaryllis, both more concerning than poinsettias. The table below uses the ASPCA plant database and other poison-control sources.</p>

