<h3>Why lilies are the real danger for cats</h3>
<p>Lilies are common in winter bouquets. According to the FDA, every part of a true lily or daylily is toxic to cats, including the leaves, petals, pollen and even the water in the vase. A cat can be poisoned just by licking pollen off its fur.</p>
<p>Early signs include drooling, vomiting, low energy and not eating. Kidney failure can follow within 24 to 72 hours. The FDA notes that if treatment is delayed by 18 hours or more, the kidney damage is generally permanent. If your cat has touched or chewed a lily, contact a vet straight away, even if your cat seems fine.</p>

<h3>Pet-safe holiday plant alternatives</h3>
<p>If you want festive greenery without the worry, these plants are listed as non-toxic to cats and dogs in the ASPCA plant database:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Christmas cactus</strong> (<em>Schlumbergera bridgesii</em>)</li>
<li><strong>Moth orchid</strong> (<em>Phalaenopsis</em>)</li>
<li><strong>African violet</strong> (<em>Saintpaulia</em>)</li>
<li><strong>Rosemary</strong> (<em>Rosmarinus officinalis</em>)</li>
</ul>
<p>Chewing a lot of any plant can still upset the stomach. Our <a href="/tools/pet-toxic-plants-guide/">pet toxic plants guide</a> covers many more species.</p>

<h2>Tips for Keeping Poinsettias and Pets Apart</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Put poinsettias up high</strong> or in a room your pet cannot enter; cats can reach most shelves.</li>
<li><strong>Check gift arrangements</strong> for lilies before bringing them inside if you have a cat.</li>
<li><strong>Offer something better to chew</strong>, like a toy or cat grass.</li>
<li><strong>Know the plant names</strong> in your home, so you can tell a vet exactly what was eaten.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/holiday-pet-safety-guide/">holiday pet safety guide</a> covers decorations and guests, and our post on <a href="/blog/thanksgiving-foods-for-dogs-and-cats/">Thanksgiving foods dogs and cats can and can’t eat</a>. Antifreeze is a far bigger winter danger than any houseplant; read about <a href="/blog/antifreeze-poisoning-in-dogs-and-cats/">antifreeze poisoning in dogs and cats</a>. If you are weighing a Christmas cactus instead, see <a href="/blog/is-christmas-cactus-poisonous-to-cats/">is Christmas cactus poisonous to cats and dogs</a>, and for wrapping and trimmings read <a href="/blog/dog-ate-tinsel/">what to do if a dog eats tinsel</a> and <a href="/blog/dog-ate-wrapping-paper/">what to do if a dog eats wrapping paper</a>.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Can a poinsettia kill a cat?</h3>
<p>It is very unlikely. Poison-control sources describe poinsettias as mildly toxic and say their danger is often exaggerated. Most cats that chew one only drool or vomit for a short time, and the signs settle on their own. Call a vet if your cat vomits repeatedly, stops eating or seems unusually tired.</p>

<h3>How much poinsettia is toxic to a dog?</h3>
<p>There is no set toxic amount for dogs. Even a small bite can cause drooling or vomiting because the sap irritates the mouth and stomach, and eating a large amount makes stomach upset more likely. Call your vet if your dog ate a lot of the plant, swallowed the pot wrapping or soil, or seems unwell afterwards.</p>

<h3>What should I do if my cat licked poinsettia sap?</h3>
<p>Wash the sap off the fur and skin with mild soap and water so your cat does not groom more of it off, then offer fresh water and watch for drooling or vomiting. Most cats are back to normal within a few hours. Call your vet if signs are severe or do not settle.</p>

<h3>Is the Christmas cactus safe for cats and dogs?</h3>
<p>The ASPCA lists the Christmas cactus (<em>Schlumbergera bridgesii</em>) as non-toxic to cats, dogs and horses, making it a good festive swap for a poinsettia. Eating a lot of any plant can still cause mild stomach upset, so keep it where pets cannot chew it.</p>

<h3>Are white or pink poinsettias any safer?</h3>
<p>No. White, pink, marbled and red poinsettias are all varieties of the same species, <em>Euphorbia pulcherrima</em>, and they contain the same irritating milky sap. Treat every colour the same way: keep it out of reach and watch your pet for drooling or vomiting if it chews one.</p>

<h3>Can touching a poinsettia hurt my pet?</h3>
<p>Brushing past an intact poinsettia is unlikely to cause harm, because the sap only escapes from broken stems and leaves. Contact with the sap can make skin red, swollen or itchy, and Pet Poison Helpline notes it rarely causes mild eye irritation. Washing the skin with mild soap and water usually helps.</p>

<h3>Should I get rid of my poinsettia if I have pets?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Most pets ignore poinsettias or stop after one bite. NC State Extension suggests that owners of pets that habitually eat plants may prefer to avoid them altogether. If your cat or dog chews houseplants often, choose a non-toxic option such as a Christmas cactus or moth orchid instead.</p>

<h3>Are poinsettias toxic to horses?</h3>
<p>Yes. The ASPCA lists the poinsettia as toxic to horses as well as to cats and dogs, with the irritant sap as the cause. After the holidays, dispose of old plants where horses and other grazing animals cannot reach them.</p>

